  "hostSuite": {
    "_": "READ THIS BEFORE YOU 'FIX' THE `vendor-unit` STEP. `node tools/verify.mjs --unit` inside the vendored copy runs twelve suites. Eleven of them are ordinary DSP and UI suites and they all pass; the twelfth, `unit`, runs the vendored `test.js`, and that file is TWO things at once — the unit's largest suite AND a conformance suite over the two hole modules a Host is required to supply. Vendored as-shipped, with the Chrome extension's own hole modules in place, `test.js` is 766 green and the whole plan is 12 of 12. With THIS Host's hole modules in place (the two paths in `ours` above, which are the only files under vendor/ this repository is allowed to differ on) it runs to its end and FAILS CLEANLY: `FAIL unit 10.6s 676 passed, 18 failed`, the runner's own `RED — 18 failing assertions` banner, exit 1. That clean failure IS the upstream stem-splitter-live#30 FIX, landed in v0.3.0: a hole module that throws while `group('host')` exercises it is now a NAMED RED (the whole-group guard; the duty-refusal probes read `settled.resolved === false && msg.includes(duty)`, so our hole modules name the duty in their refusal messages) rather than the old `TypeError: listeners[0] is not a function` at test.js:5833 that killed the file mid-run and took 103 assertions with it. The 18 reds that remain include the transport, wire and chrome-scan assertions no non-Chrome Host can satisfy, so the vendored runner exits 1 on every run, for ever, and the step would be permanently red — which AGENTS.md says a red must never become, because a red nobody can ever clear is a red everybody learns to skip. The step therefore pins the WHOLE post-swap report instead of demanding a green that cannot happen. Nothing under vendor/ is patched: CONTRIBUTING.md rule V1 forbids editing anything that is not in `ours`, and the `vendor-intact` step runs BEFORE this one and would go red the moment anybody tried.",
    "id": "unit",
    "exitCode": 1,
    "verdictLine": "RED — <n> failing assertions",
    "passingSuites": 11,
    "bothWays": "THIS IS A TWO-WAY PIN AND NOT AN IGNORE-LIST, AND THE SECOND DIRECTION IS THE POINT. `assertions` above is NOT test.js's 766 plus the rest: it is 561, the total of the ELEVEN suites that still pass, and the step requires all eleven to PASS and to sum to exactly that. It ALSO requires `unit` to be the ONLY non-PASS row AND to have FAILED CLEANLY — asserting, with its assertion counts on the row and the RED banner in the transcript. So a GREEN `unit` FAILS this step, and so does a CRASHED one. Post-swap a green `unit` cannot mean the unit got better; it can only mean the two files in `ours` are not this Host's any more, i.e. somebody restored the extension's. And a crash is not the pinned shape at all: the pinned case runs to its end and prints its own RED banner, which is the whole point of the upstream #30 fix. Watched red that way, both directions, and both mutations are named in tools/verify.mjs beside the step. Two earlier prose descriptions of this same failure were already stale by the time anybody read them, which is why every number here is checked on every run rather than merely written down.",
    "verdictFor_test_js": "NOT this step — the `conformance` step, which runs the same file, unedited, to completion under this Host's own platform (tools/suites/conformance.mjs, pinned by vendor/.conformance.json: 766 assertions, 747 passed, 19 failed, every red named and argued in docs/CONFORMANCE.md). Nothing measured there is lost here.",
    "upstreamIssue": "https://github.com/itziklerner-pag/stem-splitter-live/issues/30"
  },
  "ort": {
    "_": "THE ONNX RUNTIME DROP — 27 MB of executable wasm and ~928 KB of .mjs glue, and until this block existed NOTHING in this repository's gate hashed a byte of it. `--check`'s set comparison excludes `^extension/vendor/ort/` on purpose (the directory is gitignored and is fetched, not copied), `extension/unit.sha256` does not cover it, and neither does `vendor/upstream.sha256` — so a stale, half-written or hand-edited drop survived every step of the plan, invisible to review, on the single largest blob of executable code this product loads. It runs inside the cross-origin-isolated `app://` origin that also holds the capture ring and the verified weights, under a CSP that grants 'wasm-unsafe-eval'. M1 is `no remote code`; this is where the remote code actually is.",
    "upstreamPins": "THE UNIT PINS TWO OF THESE FOUR AND NOT THE OTHER TWO. `vendor/stem-splitter-live/tools/fetch-vendor.sh` carries a `verify` line for ort.all.bundle.min.mjs and ort-wasm-simd-threaded.jsep.wasm; it copies ort-wasm-simd-threaded.mjs and ort-wasm-simd-threaded.jsep.mjs with no hash at all, while its own comment records that the glue .mjs `is still fetched dynamically in some paths`, i.e. it is loaded and run. That is an UPSTREAM finding — rule V1 forbids patching it here — and it is recorded in docs/CONFORMANCE.md `Findings for stem-splitter-live`. This block closes it LOCALLY: all five files are pinned here, from the drop the pinned fetch script produced, and `bash tools/vendor-unit.sh --check` re-hashes every one of them offline in ~0.2 s.",
    "fetchedBy": "vendor/stem-splitter-live/tools/fetch-vendor.sh, at the pinned tag",
